## Tables in HTML in Hindi
HTML tables, covering their creation, shortcuts, structure, styling with CSS, and some advanced attributes.

### **Creating a Simple HTML Table:**

- Start with a basic HTML file named `tables.html`.
- Use shortcuts like `!` (exclamation mark) in editors for boilerplate HTML.
- Emmet shortcuts provide quick ways to generate table structures.

## Creates 
<table>
  <tr>
    <th></th>
    <th></th>
    <th></th>
  </tr>
</table>

## Using Emmet Shortcuts for Tables:
- Emmet can create table rows (tr), headings (th), and data cells (td) efficiently.
- Example: Writing table>tr>th*3 generates a table with one row and three headings.
- These shortcuts speed up coding but require memorization.
- Not all shortcuts work consistently; manual coding is sometimes necessary.

## **Table Structure Basics:**
- Tables consist of:
    - **`<table>`**: Container element.
    - **`<tr>`** (table row): Represents rows.
    - **`<th>`** (table header): Header cells, usually bold by browsers.
    - **`<td>`** (table data): Regular data cells.
    - Proper use of `<th>` vs. `<td>` is important for **SEO and accessibility**, as search engines differentiate between headings and data.
# Important
Use <th> for headings (SEO + accessibility)
Use <td> for data

## **Populating Table with Data:**
- Example columns: *Country*, *Country Code*, *Email*.
- Data cells filled with sample data like "India", "IN", and "[india@google.com](mailto:india@google.com)".
- Copying and pasting rows in editor using keyboard shortcuts like `Alt+Shift+Down` (Windows) or `Option+Shift+Down` (Mac) speeds up repetitive data entry.

### Table Structure (example)
<table>
  <tr>
    <th>Country</th>
    <th>Code</th>
    <th>Email</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>India</td>
    <td>IN</td>
    <td>india@google.com</td>
  </tr>
</table>

# Explanation
<table> → main container
<tr> → table row
<th> → header cell (bold by default)
<td> → data cell

## Styling Tables with CSS
- Basic CSS styling can be embedded within the same HTML file inside `<style>` tags.

<style>
  body {
    background-color: #313131;
    color: white;
  }

  table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
  }

  th, td {
    border: 1px solid black;
    padding: 10px;
    text-align: left;
  }

  tr:nth-child(even) {
    background-color: #444;
  }
</style>

## Key CSS properties used:
- - `width: 100%`: Table expands to full screen width. (full width table)
- - `border-collapse: collapse`: Merges border lines between cells to avoid double borders. (removes double borders)
- - `border: 1px solid black`: Adds visible borders to table and cells. (adds visible borders)

## Text alignment and color adjustments:(controls alignment)
- - Headers often bold by default.
- - Background colors added to rows using CSS pseudo-classes like `tr:nth-child(even)`.
- - Text color changed to white for better contrast on dark backgrounds.
- - Padding (e.g., `padding: 10px`) improves cell spacing and readability.(improves spacing)

